<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Enguerrand VII de Coucy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1340">1340</a>–<a href="/years/1397">1397</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Enguerrand de Coucy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Enguerrand VII de Coucy was a French nobleman and soldier, lord of one of the great baronies of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and son-in-law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-iii-of-england">Edward III of England</a>. He led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> companies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, where in 1384 he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a> and sold it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>. He was captured at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nicopolis">Battle of Nicopolis</a> in 1396 and died a prisoner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/enguerrand-vii-de-coucy.jpg" alt="Enguerrand VII de Coucy" width="320" height="555" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rs-nourse · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Enguerrand_de_Coucy,_1st_Earl_of_Bedford,_KG.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
